  that they  needed and i'm very proud of the fact  that with the nhs  we delivered on that during the pandemic  because it was critical that earlier  evidence from the prime minister's  former advisor dominic cummings loomed  large over today's proceedings  he was asked to comment earlier on mr  hancock's evidence when are you going to  um provide the evidence for the  allegations you have here to health  secretaries  so no response from dominic cummings but  his claim that he repeatedly called for  the sacking of matt hancock was brought  up today  but the health secretary quizzed over  why there appeared to be so much  animosity between the pair  i have no idea i mean i i just  you know i worked directly with the  prime minister from the start of this  and of course i worked with his um his  aides and his team as well  and um i i have no idea  did you know that he wanted the prime  minister to fire you  uh yes because he briefed the newspapers  at the time right  or somebody briefed the newspapers i now  have a better idea of who it was  and did you raise an objection to that  yes of course and i had the prime  minister's hold some support all the way  through  well let's speak to our deputy political  editor sam coats who's here  uh sam this was the health secretary's  chance to defend himself defend his  handling  of the pandemic and to come back against  those explosive claims made by dominic  cummings how did he do  well there was two parts to today  session really there was the dictate  the agenda as dictated by dominic  cummings in his explosive evidence  session  uh a few weeks ago and on those issues  as you heard really the committee didn't  get any  uh joy they didn't get any specific  answers  matt hancock rejected the claims of  dominic cummings and effectively the  committee  allowed him to move on the real meat of  today's session however were the  questions that the committee had about  the serious challenges  even perhaps the errors that the  government made in the handling of the  pandemic  and across four and a quarter hours matt  hancock was drawn out in detail  uh on those things the charge sheet  against government  even from this committee which has two  conservative chairs  appears pretty substantial the committee  fears that the country locked down too  late last year  that there wasn't the testing capacity  and probably put people back in care  homes  even though they were testing positive  from hospital  matt hancock didn't really reject those  claims outread  outright instead appeared to try and  want to explain  uh contextualize in some cases point the  finger of blame there's a lot in there  i think that will be chewed over for  some time to come one of the most  extraordinary  things he revealed was that right at the  end of january so  nearly two months before the country  locked down he got advice  that under the worst case scenario there  could be over  uh 200 000 deaths uh sorry 820 000  deaths  based on modelling let's hear what matt  hancock said he was told at the end of  january 2020  the challenge in those early weeks of  march was making a  massive judgment probably the most  significant judgment that any prime  minister has made  in certain peace time based on  incomplete information and a great pace  so  i instructed the department and the  health system  to plan on the basis of a reasonable  worst case scenario in january  in fact on the 27th of january and  on the and i asked for a reasonable  worst-case scenario planning assumption  and i was given the planning assumption  based on  spanish flu and it was signed off at  cobra  on the 31st of january and that was a  planning assumption  for 820 000 deaths  and i was determined that that would not  happen on my watch  sam this is a lessons learned committee  hearing what did the health secretary  say about where he thinks things went  wrong  well it was interesting there were lots  of different places whether blame lies  not all of